ritual motifs (such as incurved altars) align with other known Aegean 
iconography.[1]  
- It notes that “generations of scholars have debated the genuineness of both 
Nestor’s and Minos’ rings,” framing the Ring of Nestor within a long-standing 
scholarly controversy about Aegean gold signet rings and possible forgeries.[1] 
- The article mentions that the ring’s iconography may “reflect cultural 
practices from the 1920s,” a point used in the broader discussion of 
authenticity and potential modern inspirations or influences.[1]  

