## Notes for your eventual article drafting

- When you write the neutral, Wikipedia-style article, you can:
  - Use Vlachopoulos [1] for dimensions and the statement that it is one of the 
largest Aegean signet rings.
  - Use the Met essay [5] to support statements that it is a gold signet ring 
known under this name and often grouped with the Ring of Minos and Phaistos 
disk.
  - Use the Cambridge chapter [4] and Vlachopoulos [1] to present the 
authenticity debate and the current scholarly consensus (while still noting 
ongoing doubts).
  - Use the Academia/Marinatos-archives article [7] to describe how and when the
ring entered the National Archaeological Museum at Athens (offered to the 
museum; Karo’s recollection).
  - Use the Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology bibliography [10] to justify citing 
Pini 1998 and Sakellarakis 1992 as key secondary works, if you can access them 
separately.
- If you need more precise information (e.g., exact iconographic description, 
detailed dating, exact find-spot), you will likely need to consult:
  - Pini, “The ‘Ring of Nestor’,” OJA 17 (1998) 1–? [10]
  - Evans, *Palace of Minos* IV, fig. 915b, as referenced in the St. Louis 
signet-ring article. [9]
  - The full texts of the Academia iconography paper and Vlachopoulos chapter. 
[1][2]

