  "documents": [
    {"local_ref": "d1", "document_summary": "Vlachopoulos’s 2020 chapter focuses
on the Ring of Nestor within Aegean iconography and debates over authenticity. 
It describes the ring as an Aegean signet ring, provides precise measurements 
(3.29 cm by 2.18 cm), and notes that it is the fifth largest known Aegean signet
ring. The work reviews earlier doubts and allegations that the ring was a 
forgery and presents research concluding that the Ring of Nestor is authentic.",
"source_ref": "s1"},
    {"local_ref": "d2", "document_summary": "This Academia article analyzes the 
iconography of the Ring of Nestor and situates the ring within wider discussions
of Aegean Bronze Age forgeries and authenticity debates. It examines the 
decorative imagery on the ring and uses this analysis to comment on how the 
object has been perceived and evaluated in terms of genuineness.", "source_ref":
"s2"},
    {"local_ref": "d3", "document_summary": "The 2020 Nestor Bibliography is an 
annual scholarly bibliography for Aegean and related studies. It lists A. G. 
Vlachopoulos’s chapter “The Ring of Nestor and the Quest for Authenticity” 
within the volume Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography,
confirming the work’s bibliographic details and indicating that it addresses 
authenticity issues in Aegean iconography.", "source_ref": "s3"},
    {"local_ref": "d4", "document_summary": "The \"Fakes and Dubitanda\" chapter
in The Art and Archaeology of the Aegean Bronze Age discusses problematic or 
disputed artifacts, including the Ring of Nestor. It notes that the Ring of 
Nestor has long attracted suspicion as a possible forgery and that doubts about 
it have persisted; however, the author of the chapter regards the ring as 
authentic despite these ongoing concerns, using it as a case study in 
authenticity assessment.", "source_ref": "s4"},
    {"local_ref": "d5", "document_summary": "The Met Museum Perspectives essay 
on historic images of the Greek Bronze Age discusses well-known artifacts and 
their photographic documentation, including the Ring of Minos, the Ring of 
Nestor, and the Phaistos Disk. It identifies the Ring of Nestor as a gold signet
ring and associates it with early 20th-century figures such as Emile Gilliéron, 
who were involved in restorations and copies and whose activities have prompted 
questions about the authenticity of some objects.", "source_ref": "s5"},
    {"local_ref": "d6", "document_summary": "This Academia article about a gold 
ring said to be from the Acropolis of Athens compares that ring with other 
famous rings, including the Ring of Minos and the Ring of Nestor. It remarks 
that the Ring of Nestor is (or was) in the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford and treats
it alongside other high-profile Bronze Age rings whose provenance or 
authenticity has been debated.", "source_ref": "s6"},
    {"local_ref": "d7", "document_summary": "The article on the authenticity of 
the Minos Ring, based on Spyridon Marinatos’s archives, focuses on another 
artifact but includes historical information about the Ring of Nestor. It quotes
Karo recalling that the Ring of Nestor was offered to the National 
Archaeological Museum in Athens at a time when he was the only non-Greek member 
of a relevant body, thereby documenting an episode in the ring’s institutional 
history and acquisition by the museum.", "source_ref": "s7"},
    {"local_ref": "d8", "document_summary": "This journal article on the Isopata
Ring addresses epiphany scenes on Aegean signet rings and references the Ring of
Nestor within a broader comparative discussion. It notes that earlier 
scholarship, cited with a 1985 reference, offers significant comments on the 
Ring of Nestor, and it gives museum and CMS catalog information for a ring in 
the Athens National Museum (inventory number and CMS number) as part of the 
discussion on cataloguing and comparison of rings.", "source_ref": "s8"},
    {"local_ref": "d9", "document_summary": "The JSTOR article about a 
signet-ring in the City Art Museum of St. Louis examines that ring’s style and 
chronology, referencing other rings for comparison, including the Ring of 
Nestor. It cites the Ring of Nestor via Arthur Evans’s Palace of Minos (PM IV, 
fig. 915b) and uses it to illustrate the historical use of certain signet-ring 
types, noting that this type fell into disuse by the Geometric period, which 
implies a Bronze Age date for the Ring of Nestor.", "source_ref": "s9"},
    {"local_ref": "d10", "document_summary": "The Dartmouth Aegean Prehistoric 
Archaeology bibliography for Lesson 24 lists key literature on Aegean Bronze Age
topics and specifically cites Pini’s article “The ‘Ring of Nestor’” in Oxford 
Journal of Archaeology 17 (1998) and Sakellarakis’s book on the Mycenaean 
pictorial style in the National Archaeological Museum of Athens. These entries 
indicate that the Ring of Nestor is the subject of dedicated scholarly study and
is treated within discussions of Mycenaean pictorial art in the Athens museum.",
"source_ref": "s10"},
    {"local_ref": "d11", "document_summary": "This 2019 blog post about the 
Griffin Warrior of Pylos discusses the warrior’s gold signet rings and compares 
them to well-known rings such as the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos. It 
observes that the bands of the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos are very 
similar to the Griffin Warrior’s ring bands, except that the Ring of Nestor has 
disks, using this comparison to comment on stylistic parallels between different
Bronze Age gold rings.", "source_ref": "s11"}
  ],
