- **Authors / Contributors:**  
  - Dora Vassilikou (author of the book “Mycenaean Civilization”)  
  - Translators: Doniert Evely, Colin MacDonald  
  - Foreword: Yannis Galanakis  
- **Publication date (online):** 19 December 2025 (per snippet)  
- **Source type:** Scholarly monograph (Aegean archaeology) with illustrated 
figure list and photo sources  
- **Query that triggered it:** `"Ring of Nestor" "Athens National Museum" 1801 
CMS I 219`  

**Relevant content (from the snippet of the figure list):**

> “Athens, National Archaeological Museum, 1801 (CMS I, 219). AAE. 141. Signet 
ring from Mycenae (chamber tomb 91). End of 15th century BC”

**Interpretation of snippet:**

- This is a figure caption / source reference associated with a photographic 
illustration in the book:
  - It describes a **signet ring** from **Mycenae (chamber tomb 91)**.  
  - It dates it to the **end of the 15th century BC** (Late Helladic context).  
  - It gives its museum reference: **Athens, National Archaeological Museum, 
1801**.  
  - It notes the **CMS** publication: **CMS I, 219**.  
- While the book text in the snippet does not explicitly call the ring the “Ring
of Nestor,” this combination—Mycenae chamber tomb 91, NA Museum 1801, CMS I 
219—matches the identifiers used in other scholarly discussions to refer to the 
ring often labeled the “Ring of Nestor.”  
- The figure list thus serves as a **modern scholarly confirmation** that:
  - A signet ring from Mycenae chamber tomb 91 with CMS I, 219 is catalogued in 
the **National Archaeological Museum, Athens**, under **inventory number 1801**.

**Relevance to the research goal:**

- Provides an authoritative, recent scholarly cross-reference tying a specific 
physical ring (with tomb provenance and CMS number) to the Athens museum catalog
number 1801.  
- Useful for reconstructing object history and checking whether this ring is the
same as, or distinct from, the Ashmolean’s “Ring of Nestor.”

