“watch-dog” or “dragon,” which Vlachopoulos re-identifies as a scorpion. The 
abstract states that the branches of this tree host “the most populous scene in 
Aegean glyptic art,” implying numerous additional motifs in the bezel design. It
further notes that the scorpion is considered a major apotropaic symbol in 
Bronze Age Aegean iconography, and that properly identifying this creature leads
