"encyclopedic"]}, "parent_covered": ["We have a clear high‑level description 
from Vlachopoulos 2020 that the bezel’s central pictorial unit is an old ‘tree 
of the world’ whose branches carry the most populous scene in Aegean glyptic 
art, implying numerous figures arranged around/within the tree.", "We have 
reliable evidence that a guardian creature beside/associated with this tree was 
called a ‘watch‑dog’ or ‘dragon’ by Arthur Evans and re‑identified as a scorpion
by Vlachopoulos, with the scorpion argued to be a major apotropaic symbol in 
Bronze Age Aegean iconography.", "We have evidence that Marinatos & Jackson 
interpret the ring as ‘pseudo‑Minoan’ and see its iconographic program as based 
on Egyptian prototypes, positioning it within an Egyptianizing tradition rather 
than a standard Aegean one.", "We have clear statements that Marinatos & Jackson
single out a butterfly motif as central to their authenticity critique and to 
their claim that the ring’s composition is informed by Egyptological 
knowledge.", "We can summarize the main polar scholarly interpretations: (1) an 
Aegean religious/cosmological tableau centered on a world‑tree with an 
apotropaic scorpion guardian (Vlachopoulos, building on and revising Evans); (2)
a pseudo‑Minoan, Egyptianizing composition whose coherent Egyptian‑style 
program, including the butterfly, is taken to indicate modern forgery (Marinatos
& Jackson).", "We can identify at least some iconographic features that are 
central to authenticity/dating arguments: the scorpion vs ‘dragon’ guardian, the
butterfly motif, and the overall Egyptianizing coherence of the scene."], 
