Aegean-studies discourse.

These materials collectively delineate the contours of the conflict: early 
acceptance (Evans), subsequent forgery suspicions (pseudo-Minoan / Gilliéron 
hypothesis), and more recent attempts to rehabilitate authenticity using 
comparative and iconographic analysis. They also show that even among scholars 
arguing for authenticity, the object is often treated as a “dubitandum” with 
enduring doubts.

