      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Theodore Eliopoulos’ 2013 article re‑examines the 
iconography of the Ring of Nestor within the broader issue of Aegean glyptic 
forgeries (“gemmae dubitandae”). It surveys the long‑standing controversy over 
the ring’s authenticity, reviewing earlier technical, stylistic and iconographic
objections, and stresses that iconography must be assessed together with new 
comparanda and contextual evidence. Eliopoulos discusses key motifs on the ring,
including the central tree, animals or guardian creatures, human figures, and 
altars, and argues that some previously perceived oddities have acquired better 
parallels in newly discovered or re‑studied Bronze Age Aegean material. He 
highlights the depiction of incurved altars on the ring and connects them with 
other secure Aegean images of incurved altars, presenting this as an argument in
favor of the ring’s authenticity. The article also traces historiography in 
which some scholars interpreted the ring’s figure poses and composition as 
reflecting modern 1920s cultural tastes (such as dance or theatrical poses), and
confronts those claims with the expanding corpus of Bronze Age rings and 
evolving understandings of Aegean iconography. Eliopoulos situates the positions
of forgery proponents (including Marinatos and Jackson) and defenders of 
authenticity (including Vlachopoulos and Pini) within this debate, using the 
ring’s motifs and their comparanda to show how interpretations have shifted over
time.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "Andreas G. Vlachopoulos’ 2020 chapter focuses on 
documenting the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277) through 
detailed iconographic analysis and comparison with securely dated Aegean Bronze 
Age material. He notes that the ring, together with other famous pieces like the
Ring of Minos, was long treated with suspicion because of unclear or legendary 
find circumstances, leading many scholars to consider it a forgery. Vlachopoulos
argues that recent discoveries of related iconographic subjects in 
wall‑paintings, gold rings, sealstones, figurines and ivories, along with 
improved study of the techniques and craftsmanship of unique signet rings from 
the early Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC), now support a reassessment in 
favor of authenticity. Central to his iconographic argument is the guardian 
creature beside an old ‘tree of the world’, which Arthur Evans had called a 
“watch‑dog” or “dragon”. Vlachopoulos re‑identifies this creature as a scorpion 
and presents it as a major apotropaic motif in Bronze Age Aegean iconography. He
contends that recognizing the scorpion motif invalidates Evans’s concept of a 
“Minoan Dragon” and allows a coherent reading of the ring’s populous scene 
arranged around the world‑tree. The chapter treats the tree form, guardian 
creature, human figure arrangement and technical execution as consistent with 
Late Bronze Age Aegean glyptic and uses these parallels to argue against forgery
claims, including those of Marinatos and Jackson.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "Nanno Marinatos and Robert Jackson’s 2011 article in 
the Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections argues that the Ring of Nestor 
is a pseudo‑Minoan forgery whose iconographic program is grounded in Egyptian 
imagery rather than genuine Aegean Bronze Age traditions. They situate their 
analysis within a history of suspicion about the ring’s authenticity: although 
Arthur Evans had accepted it as authentic after its reported discovery in 1924, 
many subsequent scholars classified it as a forgery. Marinatos and Jackson 
examine the ring’s composition, including the central tree, guardian or hybrid 
creature, altars or architectural forms, human figure poses, and decorative 
elements, and claim that these components form a coherent Egyptianizing tableau.
A key element in their argument is a butterfly motif, which they interpret as 
central evidence of forgery because they believe its form and placement reflect 
detailed Egyptological knowledge of Egyptian decorative or symbolic motifs that 
would not have been available or deployed in authentic Aegean glyptic of the 
16th–15th century BC. They compare the ring’s motifs and compositional layout 
with specific Egyptian prototypes—such as tree‑of‑life scenes, solar imagery and
ornament that resembles butterflies—arguing that these parallels are closer than
any Bronze Age Aegean comparanda. On this basis, they maintain that the ring 
should be seen as pseudo‑Minoan, created in a modern context inspired by 
Egyptology and Egyptianized art rather than by genuine Aegean religious or 
cosmological iconography.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "Franziska Hudler’s 2019 conference paper examines 
sealstones and signet rings of doubtful authenticity (“gemmae dubitandae”) 
within Aegean Bronze Age archaeology, analyzing how such contested objects shape
methodological and iconographic debates. A section is devoted to the Ring of 
Nestor. Hudler outlines the historiographical trajectory of scholarly opinion: 
Martin Nilsson initially accepted the ring but later classified it as a ‘suspect
object’ in the second edition of his \"Minoan‑Mycenaean Religion\"; Hagen 
Biesantz’s 1954 study argued against authenticity on stylistic grounds; Agni 
Xenaki‑Sakellariou’s 1994 work offered detailed iconographic and typological 
criticism of the ring; and Ingo Pini’s 1998 article responded by using newly 
available data and parallels to defend authenticity. Hudler stresses that 
iconographic features such as the guardian creature (variously interpreted as 
dog, dragon or scorpion), the central tree, altars, figure poses and the 
composition have been repeatedly scrutinized as authenticity‑critical motifs. 
She also situates the later contributions of Marinatos and Jackson, who 
emphasize Egyptian iconography and forgery, and of Vlachopoulos, who defends an 
Aegean Bronze Age date, showing how new finds and changing interpretive 
frameworks continually reshape assessments of the ring.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "Nanno (Ourania) Marinatos’s institutional curriculum 
vitae lists her scholarly publications and research interests. Among them is the
article \"The Pseudo‑Minoan Nestor Ring and Its Egyptian Iconography\" published
in the Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections, vol. 2 (2011), co‑authored 
with Robert Jackson. The CV places this work within a broader research program 
on Minoan religion, Egyptian solar religion, and Egyptianized Aegean imagery, 
indicating that Marinatos approaches the Ring of Nestor through the lens of 
Egyptian iconography and cross‑Mediterranean religious connections. While the CV
does not describe specific motifs on the ring, it confirms bibliographic details
and underscores that the ring is treated as pseudo‑Minoan and analyzed in 
relation to Egyptian iconographic prototypes.",
      "source_ref": "s5"
    }
