      "local_ref": "e10",
      "text": "Marinatos and Jackson’s analysis emphasizes that the ring’s 
composition—combining a central tree, hybrid guardian, altar or architectural 
forms, human figure poses, and a butterfly motif—forms a coherent Egyptianizing 
program that they regard as incompatible with expectations for 16th–15th century
BC Aegean glyptic.",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["summarizes their iconographic reasoning as described in 
the source summary"],
      "limitations": ["concrete examples of Egyptian prototypes or Aegean 
counter‑examples are not given in the accessible material"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e11",
      "text": "Hudler’s paper states that Martin Nilsson originally accepted the
Ring of Nestor but reclassified it as a ‘suspect object’ in the second edition 
of \"Minoan‑Mycenaean Religion\", reflecting heightened concern about forgeries 
of Aegean glyptic.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["historiographical evidence about changes in scholarly 
opinion"],
      "limitations": ["exact quotation and page reference from Nilsson’s work 
are not provided"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e12",
      "text": "Hudler reports that Hagen Biesantz’s 1954 article rejected the 
Ring of Nestor’s authenticity chiefly on stylistic grounds, marking one of the 
early influential scholarly condemnations of the ring.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["summary of Biesantz’s position"],
      "limitations": ["no detailed enumeration of the stylistic oddities 
Biesantz identified"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e13",
      "text": "Hudler notes that Agni Xenaki‑Sakellariou’s 1994 study provided 
an extensive critique of the Ring of Nestor, focusing on iconographic details 
she regarded as suspicious and on typological and technical features that she 
considered inconsistent with genuine Aegean Bronze Age rings.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "other",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["overview of the scope and direction of 
