3. Theodore Eliopoulos, “Some Observations on the Iconography of the ‘Ring of 
Nestor’”  

- **Title**: SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’  
- **URL**: 
https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_RI
NG_OF_NESTOR_  
- **Publisher / Author**: Theodore Eliopoulos; paper hosted on Academia.edu; 
originally published in “Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle 
Missioni Italiane in Oriente” (year indicated as 2013 in the Academia metadata) 
- **Date**: 2013 (as per paper metadata on Academia.edu)  
- **Source type**: Scholarly article (peer-reviewed journal); online copy on an 
academic sharing platform  
- **Triggered by query**: `"Ring of Nestor" Ashmolean AN1938.1130 National 
Archaeological Museum Athens 1801 CMS I 219` and `"Ring of Nestor" AN1938.1130 
NAM 1801 CMS I 219`  

- **Relevance to the research question**:  
  Eliopoulos’ article is a modern scholarly re-examination of the ring’s 
iconography, written against the background of authenticity debates surrounding 
the object known as the “Ring of Nestor.” The paper addresses the ring’s 
imagery, compares it to other Minoan and Mycenaean iconographic motifs 
(including incurved altars), and explicitly situates the ring within the debate 
over forgeries and unprovenanced artifacts. This makes it highly relevant for 
understanding how current scholarship treats the “Ring of Nestor” as an object, 
including whether it is considered genuine or a forgery and how its iconography 
is interpreted. While the publicly visible metadata on Academia emphasizes 
iconography and authenticity debates, the previewed text does not specify in 
this snippet whether Eliopoulos explicitly equates or distinguishes the 
Ashmolean ring (AN1938.1130) from the National Archaeological Museum’s Mycenae 
ring (NAM 1801 / CMS I, 219); however, given the paper’s focus, it likely 
references the main “Ring of Nestor” object discussed by Evans and later 
scholars and may address misidentifications or cataloguing issues within the 
authenticity debate.  

- **Content / faithful summary**:  
  According to the abstract and key takeaways visible in the Academia listing, 
the paper offers an iconographic analysis of the “Ring of Nestor” within the 
broader context of Aegean forgeries and authenticity issues. Eliopoulos revisits
earlier interpretations of the ring’s imagery, focusing in particular on 
features such as incurved altars and ritual scenes, and uses these to argue 
about the ring’s significance in Minoan and Mycenaean art. The article 
emphasizes that iconography plays a crucial role in evaluating authenticity, 
especially where technical analyses yield contradictory results. It notes that 
“generations of scholars have debated the genuineness of both Nestor’s and 
Minos’ rings,” highlighting that the “Ring of Nestor” sits at the intersection 
of myth, art, and questions about unprovenanced antiquities. The paper argues 
that outright dismissal of such objects can overlook potentially valuable 
information, while also stressing the need for reliable reference materials. The
specific institutional catalogue numbers (such as Ashmolean AN1938.1130 or NAM 
1801) are not visible in the public preview, and there is no direct statement in
the visible text about a transfer or shared provenance between the Ashmolean and
the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.  

