7. National Archaeological Museum, Athens – “The Ring of Theseus” (contextual 
comparator)  

- **Title**: “The Ring of Theseus” – National Archaeological Museum  
- **URL**: 
https://www.namuseum.gr/en/hidden_museum/quot-the-ring-of-theseus-quot  
- **Publisher / Author**: National Archaeological Museum, Athens  
- **Date**: Posted as part of the “Unseen Museum”; page indicates the ring was 
on display 12 January – 15 March 2015; the page itself is part of the museum’s 
current website.  
- **Source type**: Museum exhibition description / official web page  
- **Triggered by query**: `"Ring of Nestor" AN1938.1130 NAM 1801 CMS I 219`  

- **Relevance to the research question**:  
  Although this page describes the “Ring of Theseus” rather than the “Ring of 
Nestor,” it is relevant as a comparator and for clarifying the National 
Archaeological Museum’s ownership and interpretation of major Mycenaean gold 
signet rings. It confirms that the NAM uses mythologically framed popular names 
(e.g., “Ring of Theseus,” “Ring of Minos”) for certain signet rings and 
distinguishes them from each other. The page specifically describes a Mycenaean 
ring found in Athens (Anafiotika), not the Mycenae chamber tomb 91 ring NAM 1801
/ CMS I, 219; however, it shows that NAM’s official communications clearly link 
particular rings to particular mythic names and provenances, reducing the 
likelihood that the museum uses “Ring of Nestor” ambiguously for multiple 
artifacts. The page does not mention the term “Ring of Nestor” or the catalogue 
number 1801, and so it does not resolve the ambiguity between Ashmolean 
AN1938.1130 and NAM 1801; nonetheless, it provides contextual evidence that NAM 
differentiates between mythic-name rings and that there is a curated practice of
naming rings in their exhibitions.  

- **Content / faithful summary**:  
  The page explains that the “Ring of Theseus” is a Mycenaean gold signet ring, 
a random find from the Anafiotika neighborhood near the Acropolis in Athens 
during construction work for the extension of the old Acropolis Museum in the 
1950s. The ring’s bezel depicts a popular Minoan bull-leaping scene, flanked by 
a lion and a tree. The text notes that Mycenaean signet rings were prestige 
objects of early Mycenaean rulers and interprets the Minoan-themed imagery as 
indicating a preference for Minoan symbolism among the Mycenaean elite. The page
further links the ring to Athenian myths about King Theseus and King Minos, 
including the story that Minos threw his signet ring into the sea and dared 
Theseus to retrieve it; the ring is positioned as a material echo of this myth. 
The page clarifies that this ring (the “Ring of Theseus”) was displayed in the 
“Unseen Museum” exhibition from 12 January to 15 March 2015. No reference is 
made to the term “Ring of Nestor” or to the Mycenae chamber tomb 91 ring (NAM 
1801 / CMS I, 219).  

---

These sources, collectively, provide:

- Authoritative confirmation and catalogue data for the Ashmolean “Ring of 
Nestor” (AN1938.1130) and its Late Minoan I dating and cult-scene iconography 
(Ashmolean Image Library; corroborated by Science Photo Library’s secondary 
description).  
- Historical context and the original popularization of the term “Ring of 
Nestor” in Arthur Evans’ 1925 monograph (Heidelberg digitization; HathiTrust 
record).  
- Modern scholarly debates around the authenticity and iconography of the 
“Nestor Ring” (Eliopoulos 2013; Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections 
article on the “Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring”).  
- Contextual evidence for the National Archaeological Museum’s practice of 
assigning mythologically framed names to Mycenaean signet rings (NAM “Ring of 
Theseus” page), which helps frame how a “Ring of Nestor” in Athens would be 
treated, even though this specific page addresses a different ring.  

None of the above online sources explicitly equate the Ashmolean “Ring of 
Nestor” AN1938.1130 with the Mycenae ring NAM 1801 / CMS I, 219 or state that 
the latter is called “Ring of Nestor” in official National Archaeological Museum
usage. They also do not document any transfer, loan, or deaccession linking the 
Ashmolean and NAM for an artifact called “Ring of Nestor.”
