4. Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections – “The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring
and Its Egyptian Iconography”  

- **Title**: The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring and Its Egyptian Iconography  
- **URL**: https://journals.librarypublishing.arizona.edu/jaei/article/id/960  
- **Publisher / Author**: Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections 
(University of Arizona); author name not in snippet but article is a scholarly 
study  
- **Date**: Not shown in snippet; Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections 
articles are generally 21st-century (exact year to be taken from article when 
accessed).  
- **Source type**: Peer-reviewed journal article  
- **Triggered by query**: `"Ring of Nestor" AN1938.1130 NAM 1801 CMS I 219`  

- **Relevance to the research question**:  
  This article directly addresses “The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring,” indicating 
that the author treats the famous “Ring of Nestor” as pseudo-Minoan (i.e., 
likely a forgery or non-authentic Minoan work) and analyses its Egyptian-looking
iconography. The snippet states that the so‑called Nestor Ring was found in 1924
and was accepted as authentic by Sir Arthur Evans, while many other scholars 
condemned it as a forgery. This places the article squarely within the 
authenticity debate that has led to the label “pseudo-Minoan.” The article is 
highly relevant to clarifying how modern scholarship treats the “Nestor Ring” as
an object, its authenticity, and how it is contextualized within Aegean and 
Egyptian art; however, the snippet does not show any explicit discussion of 
cataloguing identities such as Ashmolean AN1938.1130 or NAM 1801 / CMS I, 219. 
It therefore contributes primarily to the interpretive context and the 
distinction between authentic Mycenaean rings (like the Mycenae/NAM ring) and 
the contested Nestor ring, rather than directly resolving the catalogue-number 
ambiguity.  

- **Content / faithful summary**:  
  The article, as summarized in the search snippet, focuses on the object known 
as the “Nestor Ring,” which was reportedly found in 1924 and was championed by 
Arthur Evans as an authentic Minoan artifact. The authors describe how many 
other scholars have considered it a forgery (“pseudo-Minoan”), and they analyze 
its iconography by comparing it with Egyptian motifs, arguing that the ring’s 
imagery reflects a complex interplay of Egyptian and Aegean themes. By framing 
the object as “pseudo-Minoan,” the article situates it as problematic within the
corpus of Minoan art and explores how its stylistic and iconographic features 
diverge from securely provenanced Minoan and Mycenaean seals and rings. The 
article does not, in the visible snippet, refer to a specific museum holding or 
inventory number, but by characterizing the ring as a pseudo-Minoan artifact 
debated in scholarship, it implicitly distinguishes it from securely excavated 
and catalogued rings like the Mycenae chamber tomb 91 ring (NAM 1801 / CMS I, 
219).  

