      "document_summary": "Ashmolean Museum’s image-library search results for 
“nestor” include an entry with accession number AN1938.1130, described as a gold
signet ring from the Late Minoan I period (c. 1600–1450 BC). The catalogue 
record gives a preferred descriptive title “Signet ring” and a popular name “The
Ring of Nestor,” and identifies it as part of the Ashmolean’s collection. The 
entry provides basic object-type information and dating but does not mention any
connection to the National Archaeological Museum in Athens, to inventory number 
1801, or to CMS I, 219, and does not suggest that the ring is on loan or has 
been transferred.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "Arthur J. Evans’ 1925 monograph presents the object 
he calls “the ring of Nestor” as part of a hoard of gold signet rings and bead 
seals from Thisbê in Boeotia. He gives detailed descriptions and illustrations 
of the ring’s cultic iconography and uses it to argue for Minoan religious and 
artistic influence on mainland Greece. The work popularizes the term “Ring of 
Nestor” and treats the ring as a Minoan artifact, but it predates later museum 
accession numbers such as AN1938.1130 or NAM 1801 and does not discuss any 
institutional catalogue identities or transfers between museums.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
